Agreed. With symlinks, as below, they don't even need to move.

> Let's think through how we could handle multi-user support. Maybe each
> user has a symlinked archive dir? So something like:
>
>     maildir/archive/pat@patmaddox.com
>     maildir/archive/claude@p7x.dev
>
> Then to archive it, we don't move it into the archives dir... we symlink
> it our respective dir?
>
> First I want to see if that mechanic works.

It works, with one sharp edge. I tried it on a scratch copy of the list
with its own notmuch database (notmuch 0.40):

- A relative link archive/claude@p7x.dev/cur/<name> ->
  ../../../cur/<name> is indexed as a second filename of the same
  message. notmuch new says "No new mail", the message keeps its id
  and thread, and both paths show up under --output=files.

- path:archive/claude@p7x.dev/** matches it. The @ needs no quoting.
  A tag line like

      +archived -inbox -- path:archive/claude@p7x.dev/**

  works, and mail-tag.sh can build the path from user.primary_email,
  so each person's hook only looks at their own dir.

- Removing the link unarchives: notmuch new reports "Detected 1 file
  rename" and the message goes back to one filename.

- Archiving a whole thread is one link per message. I linked all five
  messages of this thread and path: matched all five.

- jj stores the links as real symlinks (mode 120000 in jj diff --git),
  so they travel through the mail branch like any other file.

The sharp edge: a dangling link is fatal. With a link to a missing
file, notmuch new stops with "Error reading file ...: No such file or
directory" and indexes nothing, not even unrelated new mail. I dropped
a new message into new/ next to a broken link, and it was not indexed.
notmuch does this on purpose. The source comments say it wants to be
pessimistic "so we don't lose lots of mail just because a user broke
a symlink". Since the mail branch is shared, one bad link breaks
indexing for everyone who pulls it.

A link can only dangle if its target leaves the list. The likeliest
way is a moderator dropping a message that someone already archived.
So a mail-tag.sh change like this would want a check before pushing,
e.g. "find -L maildir/archive -type l" prints nothing.

> Also want to think if there are any other ways - obviously that approach
> wouldn't scale to many users. In that case, there'd need to be some
> other mechanism, perhaps everyone maintains a separate fork / branch
> with their own archiving setup. I don't want to get too hung up on it,
> but I'd at least like to think through how to make the process scale to
> more users.

Three shapes, roughly in order of scale:

1. Per-user link dirs on the shared mail branch, as above. Adds never
   conflict, because each person writes only in their own dir. The
   cost grows with users times archived messages. Everyone carries
   everyone's archive state, every message gains a filename per
   person who archived it, the moderator merges everyone's
   archiving, and one person's dangling link breaks everyone's
   indexing. That's fine for a handful of people.

2. The same links, but on a per-user branch. Each person's archive/
   lives on their own bookmark and is never merged into mail. Their
   workspace is a merge of mail@upstream and their archive branch.
   The shared branch stays pure list, and a broken link only hurts
   its owner. The cost is a merge in every pull. This is your
   separate fork idea, and it needs no new tool.

3. Tags in git, keyed by Message-ID, with no links at all. This is
   what the notmuch project itself does, with notmuch-git (nmbug).
   It's already installed here: man notmuch-git. A bare git repo
   holds one empty file per message and tag,
   tags/<hash>/<hash>/<msgid>/<tag>, and only tags under a chosen
   prefix are synced. notmuch git commit/push/pull/checkout move
   them between the database and the repo. Because every tag is its
   own file, merges are effectively conflict-free. Since it's keyed
   by id, a missing message is reported as status "U" rather than
   breaking notmuch new. For per-user state you give each person
   their own repo or branch, or a per-user prefix (pat::archived).
   mail-tag.sh would then need one line, "-inbox -- tag:archived",
   and a rebuild becomes notmuch new followed by notmuch git
   checkout.

   The catch is your earlier constraint. The state no longer lives in
   the mail branch alone, but in a second git repo next to it. I
   haven't checked whether that repo could be a ref in the same jj
   repo, which would bring it closer.

My take: 1 is enough for now and is tested. 3 is the one built for
many users, and the notmuch community already runs it. The mailbox
format stays the same under all three, so starting with 1 doesn't
rule out moving to 2 or 3 later.

> Also maybe research how people in notmuch community do stuff like this?
> Both for personal databases, and shared ones. I know notmuch itself is
> storing their mailing list in git if I remember correctly.

Close: it's their tags that live in git, not the list.

Shared: nmbug, as in 3 above. Developers clone
https://nmbug.notmuchmail.org/git/nmbug-tags.git and share tags like
notmuch::patch, notmuch::pushed, and notmuch::obsolete to track
patches on the list. The tags are shared, not per-user. Anyone with
push access edits the same set. The list itself is on Mailman
pipermail, plus a maildir tarball at
https://nmbug.notmuchmail.org/archive/notmuch-list.tar.xz. There's an
unofficial public-inbox mirror at https://yhetil.org/notmuch/.
public-inbox is the git-backed one: one message per commit in a
single file "m", with deletes recorded as a "d" commit. notmuch can't
index it directly, though.

Personal: the usual "done" is just -inbox, or +archived. Emacs binds
"a" to it. Some people move files into an archive maildir, e.g. afew's
MailMover. notmuch sees the rename and keeps the tags. Muting a thread
is a "muted" tag plus a post-new rule that keeps new messages in that
thread out of the inbox. To carry tags between machines, people use
muchsync (mail and tags over ssh), notmuch dump/restore kept in git,
or maildir flag sync, which renames files. All of these keep state
outside the messages, which matches "movable, but immutable".

